{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over temple roofs. The air smelled of incense and matcha. I spent mornings wandering Nishiki Market, nibbling on mochi, grilled eel, and pickled plums, each bite a burst of umami magic.\n\nIn Gion, I spotted a geisha gliding down a quiet lane, her obi a ripple of silk, her wooden geta clicking like a metronome. I didn’t snap a photo—some moments aren’t meant for screens.\n\nThe train to Hiroshima was silent, save for the rhythmic clack of wheels. At the Peace Memorial Park, I stood before the Atomic Bomb Dome, humbled by silence. A child’s origami crane, tied to a string, fluttered in the breeze—a quiet protest of hope.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya Crossing was a tidal wave of people, yet no one bumped. I ate ramen at a 20-seat stall in Shinjuku, slurping noodles while the chef nodded, no words needed. At midnight, I wandered Akihabara—neon signs, anime plushies, and the faint hum of electric guitars from hidden live houses.\n\nOn my last morning, I sat on a wooden bench in Meiji Shrine’s forest, listening to wind through cedars. A monk passed, smiled, and offered a single sweet. “For peace,” he said in broken English.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—in the rustle of tatami, the precision of a tea ceremony, the way strangers bow without looking up. I came for the sights. I left with stillness.\n\nI didn’t bring back much. Just a tea set, a notebook full of scribbled haiku, and the quiet certainty that some places don’t just change your itinerary—they change your soul.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796